{0 Getting started}

Write a Cloudflare Worker entirely in OCaml. workero compiles your program to
WebAssembly with wasm_of_ocaml, and its library gives you the
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/}runtime APIs}:
requests, responses, [fetch], variables, secrets and cron triggers.

By following this guide you build a Worker, run it locally and deploy it.

{1 Prerequisites}

- {{:https://opam.ocaml.org}opam} with OCaml 5.4 or later.
- {{:https://nodejs.org}Node.js} and [npm].
- [workero], from opam: [opam install workero].
- [wasm-opt] from binaryen 119 or later on [PATH]. wasm_of_ocaml runs it. The
  [binaryen] npm package provides it, and the template lists it as a
  dependency.

{1 1. Create a new project}

Copy the [template/] directory of the repository. It is a complete project:

{@sh[
cp -r template my-worker
cd my-worker
npm install
]}

[npm install] installs Wrangler, the Cloudflare tool that runs and deploys
Workers, and [binaryen]. The project has these files:

- [dune-project]: the dune project. It depends on [workero].
- [src/dune]: builds [src/main.ml] as an executable with [(modes wasm)].
- [src/main.ml]: the Worker.
- [wrangler.toml]: the Wrangler configuration. It runs [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js]
  as the build command, and sets [GREETING] in [[vars]].
- [package.json]: [wrangler] and [binaryen].

See {{!page-template}the template} for details.

{1 2. Develop locally}

Run {{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/commands/general/#dev}[wrangler dev]}
to start a local server:

{@sh[
npx wrangler dev
]}

Wrangler runs [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js] and serves the Worker on
{{:http://localhost:8787}http://localhost:8787}. A change in [src/] triggers a
rebuild. If you have not used Wrangler before, it opens a browser to log in to
your Cloudflare account. See
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/commands/general/#login}[wrangler login]}
when you have no browser.

{@sh[
curl localhost:8787/
curl -d ping localhost:8787/echo
]}

{1 3. Write your Worker code}

The entry point is [src/main.ml]. It registers a handler with {!Workero.serve}:

{@ocaml[
let handler req env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path (Request.url req)) with
  | GET, "/" -> Response.text "Hello from OCaml on Cloudflare Workers\n"
  | POST, "/echo" -> Response.text (Request.text req)
  | GET, "/env" ->
      Response.text
        (Option.value (Env.var env "GREETING") ~default:"unset" ^ "\n")
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:404 "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{!Workero.serve} takes a function with the same three arguments as the
JavaScript [fetch(request, env, ctx)] handler. It runs in direct style, so
[Request.text] and [Workero.fetch] return values, not promises. The next pages
explain each part:

- {{!page-handlers}Handlers}: [serve], [scheduled], and the arguments of a
  handler.
- {{!page-requests}Requests and responses}: read a request, build a response,
  call other servers, read and write JSON.
- {{!page-env}Variables and secrets}.
- {{!page-async}Waiting for JavaScript}: [Promise.await] and background work.
- {{!page-logging}Logging}.

{1 4. Deploy}

Deploy to a [*.workers.dev] subdomain, or to a
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/configuration/routing/custom-domains/}Custom Domain}
if you have one. Wrangler asks you to set up a subdomain the first time.

{@sh[
npx wrangler deploy
]}

Preview the Worker at [<your-worker>.<your-subdomain>.workers.dev]. The first
deploy to a new subdomain can answer with
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/support/troubleshooting/http-status-codes/cloudflare-5xx-errors/error-523/}523}
errors until DNS propagates, which takes about a minute.

You now have an OCaml Worker deployed. To learn how it works, read
{{!page-how_it_works}How it works}. To test it, read
{{!page-testing}Testing and limits}.
